1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,613
Welcome to chapter three of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,613 --> 00:00:13,568
Last time we turned words into vectors, and found that averaging them loses order and context.

3
00:00:13,568 --> 00:00:19,339
Today we meet the fix, the single most important idea in modern AI: attention.

4
00:00:19,339 --> 00:00:35,913
We'll build the intuition with analogies, then do the actual arithmetic by hand, and then look inside a real GPT-2 model to see what attention really does, including a couple of surprises the analogies don't prepare you for.

5
00:00:36,763 --> 00:00:37,618
Where are we?

6
00:00:37,618 --> 00:00:43,996
Chapter two gave us embeddings, but static ones that can't tell dog bites man from man bites dog.

7
00:00:43,996 --> 00:00:49,256
Today, attention lets each word look at every other word and update its meaning.

8
00:00:49,256 --> 00:00:52,807
Chapter four assembles the full transformer around it.

9
00:00:52,807 --> 00:00:57,343
And in chapter ten we'll implement attention in PyTorch line by line.

10
00:00:57,343 --> 00:01:04,774
As this is part one, there's no book notebook, so the code and the GPT-2 experiments you'll see today are my own.

11
00:01:05,623 --> 00:01:06,788
Here's the plan.

12
00:01:06,788 --> 00:01:13,121
First the problem attention solved: recurrent networks and their sequential bottleneck.

13
00:01:13,121 --> 00:01:16,615
Then what attention is, through three analogies.

14
00:01:16,615 --> 00:01:19,599
Then the core trio, query, key and value.

15
00:01:19,599 --> 00:01:27,096
Then the calculation itself, step by step, including why we divide by the square root of the dimension.

16
00:01:27,096 --> 00:01:32,847
Then multi-head attention, self versus cross attention, and the quadratic cost.

17
00:01:32,847 --> 00:01:35,758
Then the 2017 paper that started it all.

18
00:01:35,758 --> 00:01:42,674
And finally real attention maps from GPT-2, the exercises, the checkpoint and review questions.

19
00:01:43,524 --> 00:01:44,573
The vocabulary.

20
00:01:44,573 --> 00:01:51,080
Attention lets each word weigh every other word by relevance and blend information from them.

21
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:57,937
Self attention works within one sequence, and cross attention lets one sequence attend to another.

22
00:01:57,937 --> 00:02:04,165
Query, key and value: what a word is looking for, what it offers, and what it hands over.

23
00:02:04,165 --> 00:02:08,293
Softmax turns scores into positive weights that sum to one.

24
00:02:08,293 --> 00:02:16,199
Scaled dot product attention computes scores as Q times K transpose, divided by the square root of the dimension.

25
00:02:16,199 --> 00:02:20,188
Multi head attention runs several attentions in parallel.

26
00:02:20,188 --> 00:02:28,164
Weights are the learned numbers, and multiplying an embedding by them is the transformation that makes Q, K and V.

27
00:02:28,164 --> 00:02:32,712
And the transformer is the attention only architecture from 2017.

28
00:02:33,562 --> 00:02:34,266
Part one.

29
00:02:34,266 --> 00:02:36,144
The problem with old AI.

30
00:02:36,993 --> 00:02:42,171
Before 2017, language models were mostly recurrent neural networks, RNNs.

31
00:02:42,171 --> 00:02:50,539
An RNN reads one word at a time, and carries a fixed size summary forward, like keeping notes on a single sticky note.

32
00:02:50,539 --> 00:02:54,936
After each word you update the note, but it never gets bigger.

33
00:02:54,936 --> 00:02:59,901
By the end of a long sentence, the early words have been squeezed out.

34
00:02:59,901 --> 00:03:03,943
In the top row you can see the note fading as it travels.

35
00:03:03,943 --> 00:03:13,446
In the bottom row, attention gives the word ran a direct line to every earlier word, so it can reach cat no matter how far back it is.

36
00:03:14,296 --> 00:03:19,885
Here's the book's example: the cat that the dog that the man owned chased ran away.

37
00:03:19,885 --> 00:03:28,570
By the time the RNN reaches ran, it has overwritten its notes with dog and man, and it's lost track of what is doing the running.

38
00:03:28,570 --> 00:03:29,580
It was the cat.

39
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:31,533
There's also a speed problem.

40
00:03:31,533 --> 00:03:41,027
An RNN can't process word ten until it has processed words one to nine, so training can't take full advantage of parallel hardware like GPUs.

41
00:03:41,877 --> 00:03:43,457
A small precision note.

42
00:03:43,457 --> 00:03:51,700
The book describes this forgetting as the vanishing gradient problem, information degrading as it travels along a chain.

43
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:54,859
That's close, but it mixes two related issues.

44
00:03:54,859 --> 00:04:02,278
Going forward, a fixed size hidden state has to compress the whole past, which is an information bottleneck.

45
00:04:02,278 --> 00:04:11,345
During training, the error signal shrinks as it's passed backward through many time steps, so long range patterns are hard to learn.

46
00:04:11,345 --> 00:04:15,398
That second one is what vanishing gradients strictly means.

47
00:04:15,398 --> 00:04:23,023
Gated networks like LSTMs and GRUs eased it a lot, but they still pass everything through one fixed size state.

48
00:04:23,873 --> 00:04:28,016
Consider the trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because it was too big.

49
00:04:28,016 --> 00:04:28,667
What is it?

50
00:04:28,667 --> 00:04:32,396
The trophy, because only a too big trophy explains not fitting.

51
00:04:32,396 --> 00:04:42,932
To work that out you need three things: keep both candidates available when you reach it, use the meaning of too big to choose between them, and connect words that are far apart.

52
00:04:42,932 --> 00:04:45,418
The old approach struggled with all three.

53
00:04:45,418 --> 00:04:48,851
We need the ability to look back at any word, at any time.

54
00:04:48,851 --> 00:04:49,857
That's attention.

55
00:04:50,707 --> 00:04:51,304
Part two.

56
00:04:51,304 --> 00:04:52,499
What is attention?

57
00:04:53,349 --> 00:04:55,468
The book gives three pictures.

58
00:04:55,468 --> 00:04:57,304
First, the cocktail party.

59
00:04:57,304 --> 00:05:05,498
In a noisy room you can focus on one conversation, yet you still notice when someone across the room says your name.

60
00:05:05,498 --> 00:05:10,372
That's selective focus, and the other voices are dimmed, not deleted.

61
00:05:10,372 --> 00:05:12,491
Second, spotlights on a stage.

62
00:05:12,491 --> 00:05:17,153
An RNN is a single spotlight that has to move from actor to actor.

63
00:05:17,153 --> 00:05:21,532
Attention is many spotlights that can light anyone, instantly.

64
00:05:21,532 --> 00:05:23,157
Third, a search engine.

65
00:05:23,157 --> 00:05:31,209
Each word runs its own search over the sentence, ranks every word by relevance, and blends together what it finds.

66
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:34,747
The key shift is from a chain to a web.

67
00:05:34,747 --> 00:05:37,985
In an RNN, each step depends on the one before.

68
00:05:37,985 --> 00:05:46,254
With attention, every pair of words is connected directly, so the first and last words can communicate in a single step.

69
00:05:46,254 --> 00:05:55,142
And because no position has to wait for another, all positions can be computed at once, which is exactly what GPUs are built for.

70
00:05:55,992 --> 00:05:56,755
Part three.

71
00:05:56,755 --> 00:05:58,211
Query, key and value.

72
00:05:59,061 --> 00:06:03,372
Imagine walking into a library looking for books about cats.

73
00:06:03,372 --> 00:06:05,312
Your question is the query.

74
00:06:05,312 --> 00:06:12,426
Each book's spine label is its key: Feline Biology, Dog Training, Cat Psychology, Medieval History.

75
00:06:12,426 --> 00:06:15,732
And the content inside each book is its value.

76
00:06:15,732 --> 00:06:24,139
You compare your query to every spine, find that Feline Biology and Cat Psychology match, and combine their contents.

77
00:06:24,139 --> 00:06:27,229
The weights in my picture are illustrative.

78
00:06:27,229 --> 00:06:31,396
The important detail is that attention's matching is soft.

79
00:06:31,396 --> 00:06:36,857
Every book gets some weight, and the mismatches just get very small weights.

80
00:06:37,707 --> 00:06:41,866
In self attention, every word plays all three roles at once.

81
00:06:41,866 --> 00:06:47,826
It's a visitor with a query, and it's also a book on the shelf with a key and a value.

82
00:06:47,826 --> 00:06:49,004
Take the cat sat.

83
00:06:49,004 --> 00:06:51,499
The word sat asks, who's doing this?

84
00:06:51,499 --> 00:06:55,587
The word cat advertises, I'm something that can do actions.

85
00:06:55,587 --> 00:07:03,280
So when sat compares its query to every key, cat scores highest, and sat pulls in a large share of cat's value.

86
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:09,656
The new representation of sat means something like sat, and by the way, the cat is doing it.

87
00:07:09,656 --> 00:07:14,437
And at the same moment, cat is running its own search, and finds sat.

88
00:07:15,287 --> 00:07:18,144
Where do queries, keys and values come from?

89
00:07:18,144 --> 00:07:20,092
They aren't stored separately.

90
00:07:20,092 --> 00:07:29,700
They're computed on the fly from the same embedding, by multiplying it with three different learned weight matrices, three lenses on the same photo.

91
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:31,713
Here's the book's tiny example.

92
00:07:31,713 --> 00:07:46,190
If cat's embedding is two, four, one, and one column of the query weights is a half, a quarter and one, then that query entry is two times a half, plus four times a quarter, plus one times one: one plus one plus one, three.

93
00:07:46,190 --> 00:07:53,136
A real query has many such entries, each with its own weights, and keys and values have their own matrices.

94
00:07:53,136 --> 00:07:55,928
None of these weights are designed by hand.

95
00:07:55,928 --> 00:07:56,967
They're learned.

96
00:07:57,817 --> 00:08:01,781
This also decodes a phrase you see in the news: open weights.

97
00:08:01,781 --> 00:08:07,174
Weights are all those learned numbers, including the query, key and value matrices.

98
00:08:07,174 --> 00:08:13,478
When a company releases an open weights model, like Llama or Mistral, it publishes those numbers.

99
00:08:13,478 --> 00:08:18,546
Then anyone can run the model on their own machine, offline, and fine tune it.

100
00:08:18,546 --> 00:08:23,225
Model code without the weights is like a recipe without the ingredients.

101
00:08:24,075 --> 00:08:24,794
Part four.

102
00:08:24,794 --> 00:08:27,168
The attention calculation itself.

103
00:08:28,018 --> 00:08:30,036
Let's walk the book's example.

104
00:08:30,036 --> 00:08:35,283
The sentence is the cat sat on the mat, and we're computing attention for sat.

105
00:08:35,283 --> 00:08:39,655
Step one: compare sat's query with every key using a dot product.

106
00:08:39,655 --> 00:08:51,291
The book's illustrative scores are point one for the, point eight for cat, point four for sat itself, point two for on, point one for the second the, and point five for mat.

107
00:08:51,291 --> 00:08:52,502
Step two: softmax.

108
00:08:52,502 --> 00:08:58,623
Exponentiate each score and divide by the total, so they become positive and add up to one.

109
00:08:58,623 --> 00:09:08,577
I recomputed it and the book's numbers are right: point one three, point two five, point one seven, point one four, point one three, point one nine.

110
00:09:08,577 --> 00:09:10,999
Cat gets a quarter of the attention.

111
00:09:11,849 --> 00:09:13,303
Here's softmax by hand.

112
00:09:13,303 --> 00:09:15,831
For each score you take e to that score.

113
00:09:15,831 --> 00:09:22,214
e to the point eight is about two point two three, and e to the point one is about one point one one.

114
00:09:22,214 --> 00:09:26,512
Add up all six exponentials and you get about eight point eight two.

115
00:09:26,512 --> 00:09:32,453
So cat's weight is two point two three divided by eight point eight two, about point two five.

116
00:09:32,453 --> 00:09:38,963
The exponential amplifies differences between scores, and the division makes the weights add up to one.

117
00:09:39,813 --> 00:09:40,570
Step three.

118
00:09:40,570 --> 00:09:44,969
Multiply each word's value vector by its weight and add them up.

119
00:09:44,969 --> 00:09:52,531
The output is a brand new vector for sat, carrying context from the whole sentence, mostly from cat, then mat.

120
00:09:52,531 --> 00:10:00,229
And because the output has the same shape as the input, we can stack many attention layers on top of each other.

121
00:10:01,079 --> 00:10:03,049
And that's the whole formula.

122
00:10:03,049 --> 00:10:07,666
Scores are Q times K transpose: every query compared with every key.

123
00:10:07,666 --> 00:10:12,418
Weights are the softmax of the scores divided by the square root of d.

124
00:10:12,418 --> 00:10:16,153
And the output is weights times V, blending the values.

125
00:10:16,153 --> 00:10:18,054
Compare, normalise, combine.

126
00:10:18,054 --> 00:10:25,591
Because we do it for all the words at once using matrix multiplication, it's extremely fast on modern hardware.

127
00:10:25,591 --> 00:10:28,578
We'll implement exactly this in chapter ten.

128
00:10:29,428 --> 00:10:31,817
Why divide by the square root of d?

129
00:10:31,817 --> 00:10:33,523
I ran a quick experiment.

130
00:10:33,523 --> 00:10:49,628
For random query and key vectors, the dot product's typical size grows like the square root of the dimension: a standard deviation of about two for d of four, about eight for d of sixty four, and twenty eight for d of seven sixty eight.

131
00:10:49,628 --> 00:10:53,996
Feed scores that big into softmax and it becomes all or nothing.

132
00:10:53,996 --> 00:10:59,660
On the left, one key gets a weight of one point zero and everything else gets zero.

133
00:10:59,660 --> 00:11:03,073
That kills learning, because the gradients vanish.

134
00:11:03,073 --> 00:11:13,582
Divide by the square root of d, and the spread is back to about one at any size, so softmax stays soft, like turning down a microphone before it distorts.

135
00:11:14,432 --> 00:11:15,169
Part five.

136
00:11:15,169 --> 00:11:16,715
Multi head attention.

137
00:11:17,565 --> 00:11:19,121
Why more than one head?

138
00:11:19,121 --> 00:11:24,129
Look at the word won in the tall lawyer who argued the case won the trial.

139
00:11:24,129 --> 00:11:33,198
To understand it, won needs its subject, lawyer, its object, trial, the modifier on the subject, tall, and the earlier action, argued.

140
00:11:33,198 --> 00:11:41,927
A single attention distribution has to share out one unit of weight, so it can't focus sharply on all four relationships at once.

141
00:11:42,777 --> 00:11:45,644
So we run several attentions in parallel.

142
00:11:45,644 --> 00:11:52,705
Each head has its own query, key and value weights, so it can learn a different kind of relationship.

143
00:11:52,705 --> 00:12:00,885
In GPT-2 small, the seven sixty eight dimensional input is projected into twelve heads of sixty four dimensions each.

144
00:12:00,885 --> 00:12:10,953
Each head runs attention separately, the outputs are concatenated back to seven sixty eight, and a final learned projection mixes them together.

145
00:12:10,953 --> 00:12:18,014
Think of it as a committee of experts: one for grammar, one for meaning, one for position, and so on.

146
00:12:18,864 --> 00:12:20,805
Do the heads really specialise?

147
00:12:20,805 --> 00:12:26,503
Here are four heads from layer five of the real GPT-2 model, all on the cat sat on the mat.

148
00:12:26,503 --> 00:12:28,131
They're visibly different.

149
00:12:28,131 --> 00:12:35,833
Some put nearly everything on the very first token, some look at the previous token, and others spread their attention out.

150
00:12:35,833 --> 00:12:37,774
Nobody designed these patterns.

151
00:12:37,774 --> 00:12:40,968
They emerged from training on next word prediction.

152
00:12:40,968 --> 00:12:45,288
The upper triangle is empty because GPT-2 can't look at future words.

153
00:12:45,288 --> 00:12:46,853
More on that in a moment.

154
00:12:47,703 --> 00:12:50,174
How many heads do real models use?

155
00:12:50,174 --> 00:12:55,407
The original 2017 transformer base had eight heads of width five twelve.

156
00:12:55,407 --> 00:13:09,723
GPT-2 small has twelve, GPT-3 has ninety six with a model width of twelve thousand two hundred and eighty eight, and Llama three point one four oh five B has a hundred and twenty eight query heads.

157
00:13:09,723 --> 00:13:15,101
These are from the published papers and model cards, not the book's table.

158
00:13:15,101 --> 00:13:17,862
Larger models tend to have more heads.

159
00:13:17,862 --> 00:13:27,164
Many modern models also let several query heads share keys and values, which is called grouped query attention and saves memory.

160
00:13:28,014 --> 00:13:28,692
Part six.

161
00:13:28,692 --> 00:13:31,555
Self attention versus cross attention.

162
00:13:32,405 --> 00:13:37,722
In self attention, the queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence.

163
00:13:37,722 --> 00:13:41,510
Each word asks what in my own sentence is relevant to me.

164
00:13:41,510 --> 00:13:43,703
That's what GPT style models use.

165
00:13:43,703 --> 00:13:49,817
In cross attention, the queries come from one sequence and the keys and values from another.

166
00:13:49,817 --> 00:13:58,389
In translation, for example, each word being generated in the target language asks what in the source sentence is relevant to me.

167
00:13:59,239 --> 00:14:00,674
One important detail.

168
00:14:00,674 --> 00:14:10,582
GPT models are trained to predict the next word, so they can't be allowed to look at the words after the one they're predicting, or they'd cheat.

169
00:14:10,582 --> 00:14:14,682
A causal mask blocks attention to any position to the right.

170
00:14:14,682 --> 00:14:19,465
So row i of the attention matrix only has entries for columns up to i.

171
00:14:19,465 --> 00:14:23,702
That's the empty upper triangle you saw in the GPT-2 heatmaps.

172
00:14:23,702 --> 00:14:26,503
We'll implement this mask in chapter ten.

173
00:14:27,353 --> 00:14:28,710
Where is each used?

174
00:14:28,710 --> 00:14:36,276
Self attention powers GPT style language models, encoding a single document, and BERT style understanding.

175
00:14:36,276 --> 00:14:51,051
Cross attention appears whenever two sequences meet: translation, image captioning where words attend to image regions, question answering, and text to image generation where the image attends to the prompt.

176
00:14:51,051 --> 00:15:01,115
The classic encoder decoder transformer uses both: self attention inside the encoder and the decoder, and cross attention connecting the two.

177
00:15:01,115 --> 00:15:05,683
This book focuses on self attention, since that's what GPT uses.

178
00:15:06,533 --> 00:15:08,007
Attention has a price.

179
00:15:08,007 --> 00:15:14,304
Every word compares itself with every other word, so a thousand tokens means a million scores.

180
00:15:14,304 --> 00:15:18,457
Double the length, and you get four times the work and memory.

181
00:15:18,457 --> 00:15:27,099
That quadratic growth is why language models have context limits, and why there's so much research into more efficient attention.

182
00:15:27,949 --> 00:15:29,145
Part seven.

183
00:15:29,145 --> 00:15:31,319
The 2017 revolution.

184
00:15:32,169 --> 00:15:45,638
In June 2017, eight researchers at Google, Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan Gomez, Lukasz Kaiser and Illia Polosukhin, published Attention Is All You Need.

185
00:15:45,638 --> 00:15:49,524
Attention already existed, as a helper that improved RNNs.

186
00:15:49,524 --> 00:16:00,514
Their radical step was to throw the recurrence away entirely and build the model from attention alone, stacked in layers, six in the encoder and six in the decoder.

187
00:16:00,514 --> 00:16:02,591
They called it the transformer.

188
00:16:03,441 --> 00:16:04,447
Why did it win?

189
00:16:04,447 --> 00:16:09,144
It trained much faster, because nothing had to wait for anything else.

190
00:16:09,144 --> 00:16:21,958
It was better, scoring twenty eight point four BLEU on the standard English to German translation benchmark, more than two points above the previous best, which is a big margin in that field.

191
00:16:21,958 --> 00:16:24,642
And it connected distant words directly.

192
00:16:24,642 --> 00:16:34,504
Two more ingredients made it work: positional encodings, to tell the model about word order now that there's no sequence, and stacking many layers.

193
00:16:34,504 --> 00:16:36,651
Both are coming in chapter four.

194
00:16:37,501 --> 00:16:38,513
One correction.

195
00:16:38,513 --> 00:16:44,587
The book says the two BLEU gain came with training that took twelve hours instead of days.

196
00:16:44,587 --> 00:16:49,176
In the paper, twelve hours is for the base model on eight P100 GPUs.

197
00:16:49,176 --> 00:16:56,262
The twenty eight point four BLEU record came from the big model, which trained for three and a half days.

198
00:16:56,262 --> 00:17:09,017
The fair summary is that the transformer reached a new record at a fraction of the training cost of earlier models, but the record itself took about three and a half days, not twelve hours.

199
00:17:09,017 --> 00:17:11,244
I've put this in the errata file.

200
00:17:12,094 --> 00:17:14,783
And then the floodgates opened.

201
00:17:14,783 --> 00:17:16,778
GPT-1 and BERT in 2018.

202
00:17:16,778 --> 00:17:22,068
GPT-2 in 2019, the model we'll load and study in this course.

203
00:17:22,068 --> 00:17:27,706
GPT-3 with a hundred and seventy five billion parameters in 2020.

204
00:17:27,706 --> 00:17:29,093
ChatGPT in 2022.

205
00:17:29,093 --> 00:17:31,175
And rapid scaling since.

206
00:17:31,175 --> 00:17:33,690
All built on the transformer.

207
00:17:33,690 --> 00:17:37,940
The title is a little tongue in cheek, of course.

208
00:17:37,940 --> 00:17:46,526
You also need embeddings, layer normalisation, feed forward layers and more, which is chapter four.

209
00:17:46,526 --> 00:17:48,781
But attention is the star.

210
00:17:49,631 --> 00:17:50,363
Part eight.

211
00:17:50,363 --> 00:17:52,426
Let's look inside a real model.

212
00:17:53,276 --> 00:17:59,905
This is real attention from GPT-2 small, averaged over all twelve heads in layers five to eight.

213
00:17:59,905 --> 00:18:01,562
Look at the row for sat.

214
00:18:01,562 --> 00:18:05,014
It doesn't look like the book's tidy illustration.

215
00:18:05,014 --> 00:18:14,197
Seventy eight percent of sat's attention goes to the very first token, the, fourteen percent goes to cat and eight percent to itself.

216
00:18:14,197 --> 00:18:18,823
And it can't see on, the or mat at all, because they come after it.

217
00:18:18,823 --> 00:18:23,242
Of what remains after that first token, cat does get most of it.

218
00:18:23,242 --> 00:18:27,937
So the subject verb pattern is there, just alongside something else.

219
00:18:28,787 --> 00:18:30,865
Two lessons the analogies hide.

220
00:18:30,865 --> 00:18:32,340
First, causal masking.

221
00:18:32,340 --> 00:18:42,061
In the book's example, sat gives nineteen percent of its attention to mat, but in a GPT style model, mat hasn't happened yet when we process sat.

222
00:18:42,061 --> 00:18:47,291
That pattern fits a bidirectional model like BERT, which sees both directions.

223
00:18:47,291 --> 00:18:51,313
The book's numbers are illustrative, so this isn't an error.

224
00:18:51,313 --> 00:18:53,593
Just keep the distinction in mind.

225
00:18:53,593 --> 00:18:55,202
Second, attention sinks.

226
00:18:55,202 --> 00:18:58,889
Many heads put most of their weight on the first token.

227
00:18:58,889 --> 00:19:09,079
Researchers think the model uses it as a kind of parking spot when nothing in particular is relevant, since softmax forces the weights to add up to one.

228
00:19:09,929 --> 00:19:13,009
I tried the book's pronoun exercise on GPT-2.

229
00:19:13,009 --> 00:19:17,937
The cake in the freezer because it was warm, versus because it was cold.

230
00:19:17,937 --> 00:19:29,231
The attention from it was exactly identical in both sentences: about point zero four on cake and point zero five four on freezer, averaged over every head and layer.

231
00:19:29,231 --> 00:19:30,394
That's not a bug.

232
00:19:30,394 --> 00:19:36,759
Warm and cold come after it, so under the causal mask they're invisible when it is processed.

233
00:19:36,759 --> 00:19:49,627
To see the attention flip the way the book describes, use a bidirectional model like BERT in a tool such as BertViz, or look at attention from the later words back toward cake and freezer.

234
00:19:49,627 --> 00:19:55,513
This is a great example of why it helps to know which kind of model you're inspecting.

235
00:19:56,363 --> 00:19:57,216
Part nine.

236
00:19:57,216 --> 00:19:57,984
Hands on.

237
00:19:58,834 --> 00:20:00,849
Exercise zero needs no computer.

238
00:20:00,849 --> 00:20:04,123
Take the chef who prepared the meal forgot the salt.

239
00:20:04,123 --> 00:20:11,491
Draw arrows from forgot to the words it should attend to: thick arrows to chef, who forgot, and to salt, forgot what.

240
00:20:11,491 --> 00:20:16,213
Then do the same for prepared: chef, who prepared, and meal, prepared what.

241
00:20:16,213 --> 00:20:22,132
They share a subject but have different objects, so each word needs its own attention pattern.

242
00:20:22,132 --> 00:20:24,839
That's exactly what per word queries allow.

243
00:20:25,689 --> 00:20:30,027
Exercise two compares heads on the lawyer who argued the case won.

244
00:20:30,027 --> 00:20:37,059
Look for one head linking won to lawyer, another linking argued to case, and another linking who to lawyer.

245
00:20:37,059 --> 00:20:46,194
Exercise three tests long range links: the cat, which had been sitting on the mat for hours while the dog barked outside, finally stood up.

246
00:20:46,194 --> 00:20:48,100
Does stood reach back to cat?

247
00:20:48,100 --> 00:20:59,535
While you explore, look for heads that track grammar, heads that just look at the previous word, heads that reach far back, and how patterns change from early to late layers.

248
00:20:59,535 --> 00:21:04,595
BertViz is the classic tool, and there are many demos on Hugging Face Spaces.

249
00:21:05,445 --> 00:21:10,830
Here's a complete attention calculation in about a dozen lines of PyTorch.

250
00:21:10,830 --> 00:21:14,396
This is my own snippet, not from the book's repo.

251
00:21:14,396 --> 00:21:17,598
Six words with eight dimensional embeddings.

252
00:21:17,598 --> 00:21:20,872
Three random weight matrices make Q, K and V.

253
00:21:20,872 --> 00:21:25,602
The scores are Q times K transpose over the square root of eight.

254
00:21:25,602 --> 00:21:32,297
A causal mask sets every future position to minus infinity, so softmax gives it zero weight.

255
00:21:32,297 --> 00:21:39,646
Softmax normalises each row, and weights times V gives the output: six new eight dimensional vectors.

256
00:21:39,646 --> 00:21:42,047
Every row of weights sums to one.

257
00:21:42,897 --> 00:21:43,607
Part ten.

258
00:21:43,607 --> 00:21:45,500
The checkpoint exercise.

259
00:21:46,350 --> 00:21:48,446
The checkpoint has three parts.

260
00:21:48,446 --> 00:21:52,299
Part one: the cat chased the mouse because it was hungry.

261
00:21:52,299 --> 00:21:53,787
What does it refer to?

262
00:21:53,787 --> 00:21:55,747
Then change hungry to scared.

263
00:21:55,747 --> 00:22:02,305
Draw the arrows from it, and notice that your human answer depends on a word that comes after it.

264
00:22:02,305 --> 00:22:07,578
Part two: design three heads for the quick brown fox jumped over the lazy dog.

265
00:22:07,578 --> 00:22:10,891
What should jumped, quick and dog each attend to?

266
00:22:10,891 --> 00:22:12,243
Part three: reflect.

267
00:22:12,243 --> 00:22:17,178
Why is attention better than strictly left to right reading for pronouns?

268
00:22:17,178 --> 00:22:18,463
Why use many heads?

269
00:22:18,463 --> 00:22:21,437
And what happens if every weight is uniform?

270
00:22:22,287 --> 00:22:23,072
My answers.

271
00:22:23,072 --> 00:22:26,922
Part one: with hungry, it is the cat, the one chasing.

272
00:22:26,922 --> 00:22:28,990
With scared, it is the mouse.

273
00:22:28,990 --> 00:22:36,120
In a bidirectional view, it should attend strongly to both candidates and to the deciding adjective.

274
00:22:36,120 --> 00:22:46,030
In GPT-2 I measured it attending to cat at point zero three seven and mouse at point zero five six, before the adjective has even appeared.

275
00:22:46,030 --> 00:22:49,096
Part two: jumped looks to fox, its subject.

276
00:22:49,096 --> 00:22:52,019
Quick looks to fox, the noun it modifies.

277
00:22:52,019 --> 00:22:55,157
Dog looks to lazy, its modifier, or to over.

278
00:22:55,157 --> 00:23:08,489
Part three: attention gives direct access to any word, many heads capture many relationship types at once, and uniform weights would just be averaging, the exact problem from chapter two.

279
00:23:09,339 --> 00:23:10,193
Part eleven.

280
00:23:10,193 --> 00:23:11,046
Wrapping up.

281
00:23:11,896 --> 00:23:13,225
The key takeaways.

282
00:23:13,225 --> 00:23:19,206
One: RNNs pass a fixed size state along a chain, so long range information fades.

283
00:23:19,206 --> 00:23:24,006
Two: attention lets every word look at every other word directly.

284
00:23:24,006 --> 00:23:30,651
Three: query, key and value mean what I'm looking for, what I offer, and what I hand over.

285
00:23:30,651 --> 00:23:37,149
Four: scores are Q K transpose over root d, then softmax, then a weighted sum of values.

286
00:23:37,149 --> 00:23:42,096
Five: multi head attention captures many relationships in parallel.

287
00:23:42,096 --> 00:23:53,615
Six: self attention works within a sequence and cross attention between sequences, GPT adds a causal mask, and the cost grows with the square of the length.

288
00:23:53,615 --> 00:23:58,119
Seven: in 2017 the transformer removed recurrence altogether.

289
00:23:58,969 --> 00:24:01,601
My notes on the book for this chapter.

290
00:24:01,601 --> 00:24:06,102
The softmax example and the query entry example are both correct.

291
00:24:06,102 --> 00:24:07,972
Two items go in the errata.

292
00:24:07,972 --> 00:24:15,727
The twelve hours figure refers to the base model, while the record setting big model took three and a half days.

293
00:24:15,727 --> 00:24:21,890
And RNN forgetting isn't strictly the vanishing gradient problem, as I explained earlier.

294
00:24:21,890 --> 00:24:33,801
Finally, a clarification rather than an error: the illustrative sat to mat weight and the pronoun exercise assume a model that can see both directions, while GPT is causal.

295
00:24:34,651 --> 00:24:36,925
Review questions with my answers.

296
00:24:36,925 --> 00:24:39,131
One: what problem did RNNs have?

297
00:24:39,131 --> 00:24:49,675
They pass a fixed size state step by step, like a sticky note that keeps getting overwritten, so early words fade, and they can't be trained in parallel.

298
00:24:49,675 --> 00:24:54,430
Two: the cocktail party analogy captures soft, relevance based focus.

299
00:24:54,430 --> 00:24:58,910
You tune in to what matters, and the rest is dimmed, not removed.

300
00:24:58,910 --> 00:25:05,319
Three: query, key and value are the search question, the spine label and the book's contents.

301
00:25:05,319 --> 00:25:12,831
Four: the three steps are dot product scores, softmax of the scaled scores, and a weighted sum of the values.

302
00:25:13,681 --> 00:25:15,662
Five: why divide by root d?

303
00:25:15,662 --> 00:25:25,124
Because dot products grow like the square root of the dimension, and huge scores make softmax one hot, which kills the gradients.

304
00:25:25,124 --> 00:25:27,911
Scaling keeps the variance around one.

305
00:25:27,911 --> 00:25:29,378
Six: why many heads?

306
00:25:29,378 --> 00:25:40,087
Each head learns its own relationship, subject, object, modifier, position or long range, and a single softmax can't focus on all of them at once.

307
00:25:40,087 --> 00:25:45,074
Seven: self attention takes Q, K and V from one sequence, as in GPT.

308
00:25:45,074 --> 00:25:53,436
Cross attention takes queries from one sequence and keys and values from another, as in translation or captioning.

309
00:25:53,436 --> 00:26:02,091
Eight: the 2017 paper removed recurrence, which brought parallel training, better quality and direct long range links.

310
00:26:02,091 --> 00:26:05,025
It's the foundation of every modern LLM.

311
00:26:05,875 --> 00:26:07,060
Your practice list.

312
00:26:07,060 --> 00:26:09,368
Recompute the book's softmax by hand.

313
00:26:09,368 --> 00:26:13,860
Run my twelve line attention, then remove the mask and compare the rows.

314
00:26:13,860 --> 00:26:21,034
Try BertViz with a BERT model on the cake and freezer sentences, and see whether it flips between cake and freezer.

315
00:26:21,034 --> 00:26:22,156
Do the checkpoint.

316
00:26:22,156 --> 00:26:28,644
And peek ahead at the book's repository, where the chapter ten notebook implements all of this properly.

317
00:26:28,644 --> 00:26:33,011
The code in this video is my own, since part one has no book notebook.

318
00:26:33,861 --> 00:26:35,230
That's chapter three.

319
00:26:35,230 --> 00:26:37,967
We now have the key innovation, attention.

320
00:26:37,967 --> 00:26:40,183
But attention alone isn't a model.

321
00:26:40,183 --> 00:26:45,854
If every word is processed in parallel, how does the model know the order of the words?

322
00:26:45,854 --> 00:26:48,005
And what happens after attention?

323
00:26:48,005 --> 00:26:58,629
Chapter four, the transformer architecture, adds positional encodings, feed forward layers, layer normalisation and residual connections, and puts it all together.

324
00:26:59,479 --> 00:27:04,775
This video is based on the book Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

325
00:27:04,775 --> 00:27:14,397
Everything here is my own adaptation for study, so please support the author and buy the book at leanpub dot com slash First LLM.

326
00:27:14,397 --> 00:27:21,109
The companion code is at github dot com slash First LLM slash code, under the MIT license.

327
00:27:21,109 --> 00:27:26,331
The GPT-2 experiments used OpenAI's released weights via Hugging Face.

328
00:27:26,331 --> 00:27:29,985
Thanks for watching, and see you in chapter four.

